@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: the mechanism of the topology strip — its inputs, its budget, its clock
' covers: internal/choiceui/topology.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/layout.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/anim.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/pen.go
' covers: internal/run/topology.go
' This file owns the MECHANISM: what the strip is a function of, where its rows come
' from, and what makes it tick. It owns no pictures.
'   the pictures, per selection ... _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-states.puml
'   the regions it is drawn in ... _spec/internal/choiceui/viewport.puml
'   the width budget ............ this file, the notes on COLS
'   how the four were chosen .... _spec/_plans/choiceui-viewport-proposals.puml
'   the shipped layout it joins .... _spec/internal/choiceui/wireframe.puml
'   the render contract it extends . _spec/internal/choiceui/choice-prompt-render.puml
'   what the tiers actually do ..... _spec/internal/egress/egress-tiers.puml
'   why the sbx row is LOCKED ...... _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
'   the key on the sbx backend ..... _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the key on the docker backend .. _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE CLOCK: MOTION IS BOUND TO CHANGE, NOT TO TIME ──────────────────────
' ── WHERE THE VOCABULARY LIVES, AND WHY IT IS NOT IN choiceui ──────────────
' topologyOf (internal/run/topology.go) is the ONLY place the picture learns
' what "allow-all" or "docker (sandbox)" mean. internal/choiceui owns the
' geometry and the glyphs and stays ignorant of the vocabulary, so a new tier
' renames a string there and redraws nothing.
'
' BOTH the egress and credentials rows need a fallback, because applicableRows
' drops any axis with one option — and cursor declares exactly one of each.
' Reading the form alone drew a "mitm + squid" hop for a run whose plan is a
' bare bridge network with no sidecar: the strip inventing a boundary that does
' not exist is the worst thing this picture can do.
'
' THE LANE COUNTS ARE ILLUSTRATIVE, NOT MEASURED. A real "how many hosts
' survive" would mean reading the Kit allowlist (harness capabilities plus
' detected providers) or Squid's config at prompt time, which is a bigger change
' than the picture is worth — so the captions say what the tier DOES and never
' claim a number. One lane always survives: on the strictest posture the agent
' still reaches its model (deny-all is the absence of an allow rule and the Kit
' can only add), so a frame with no lane would say the agent cannot think.
'
' The UNREADABLE sbx baseline is drawn as deny-all, deliberately. proveo could
' not read the host policy, so it may be anything; drawing the most permissive
' posture and captioning it "the host allows every destination" would state as
' fact the one thing nobody measured. An unknown boundary is the tightest one.
'
' focusOf never focuses a LOCKED row, because `move` skips it — so on an sbx
' harness the hop is simply never the highlighted element. That is honest: the
' row is locked precisely because there is nothing there to choose.
'
' A keystroke runs the filmstrip for animWindow and the strip then rests on its
' still frame, so any movement on screen means something in the form actually
' moved — the only thing that makes the motion worth the distraction it costs.
'
' PollEvent blocks, so the clock cannot be a timer the draw loop reads; it has
' to be an event. PostEvent is non-blocking and reports a full queue rather than
' parking, so a tick arriving against a screen nobody is reading is dropped
' instead of wedging the goroutine that sent it.
'
' Both times are DURATIONS off one monotonic reading, never wall-clock instants.
' A wall clock stepped backwards by NTP makes an elapsed time negative, and a
' negative elapsed never satisfies "the window has closed" — so the strip would
' repaint every 120ms forever with no pulse to show for it. One last post fires
' as the window closes, so the final frame painted is the still one rather than
' a mid-animation frame with the mote frozen on a lane.
'
' bump is called once per KEY EVENT rather than per mutation: the cursor changes
' the strip's emphasis and `move` does not report through OnChange, so hanging
' it off individual mutations would leave the one case that matters uncovered.
' It posts nothing itself — the keystroke already causes a repaint. stop WAITS
' for the goroutine to leave so no post is in flight against a screen the caller
' is tearing down; Run registers it after screen.Fini precisely so it runs first.
'
' ── WHY THE STRIP HAS ITS OWN WRITER ───────────────────────────────────────
' pen advances by DISPLAY width rather than rune count, because the strip is
' assembled from variable-width pieces — a devicon here, a label of unknown
' length there — so no caller can precompute the columns the way the row painter
' does. `put` is deliberately left alone: its callers do their own arithmetic
' (`x += len(glyph) + len(opt) + 3`), and making it width-aware without
' rewriting them would only let the two disagree.
'
' A zero-width rune is attached to the PRECEDING cell as a tcell combining rune
' instead of being given a column, and it is asked directly rather than through
' go-runewidth — which reports U+FE0F, the variation selector, as ONE column.
' That single disagreement is why emoji are kept out of the strip entirely: a
' writer trusting the width table smears every variation-selected glyph one cell
' right. The mark attaches to p.x-1's BASE cell, never to the continuation cell
' of a wide rune, which tcell owns.
'
' textWidth is asked rather than assumed: a Nerd Font devicon lives in the
' private-use area, which Unicode classes as AMBIGUOUS width — one column in a
' Latin locale, two in a CJK one — so the figure's budget has to be measured the
' same way tcell will measure it when it paints.
'
' ── WHY THE FRAME IS A PROJECTION, AND WHAT ITS ZERO VALUE DRAWS ───────────
' Frame is one still picture of the run the form currently describes: where the
' credential rests, which hop is in the path, how many lanes survive it. It is
' built at paint time from the same values Selection returns, so it cannot
' disagree with the checkboxes above it. Everything the design calls a
' "re-label" is a STRING here rather than a shape, which holds the picture to
' one geometry.
'
' THE ZERO VALUE IS THE SCREENED HOP, deliberately. A zero Frame — one built by
' a caller that forgot a field, or by a future bug — draws the tighter picture
' with no lanes rather than an open one: a security figure must not fail to the
' most permissive reading of itself.
'
' ── WHY THE GLYPHS ARE DEVICONS AND NOT EMOJI ──────────────────────────────
' Emoji read well in a diagram and badly in a grid. The speaking head is two
' runes that go-runewidth measures as one column and terminals draw as two, and
' the cloud is an ambiguous-width character whose column count depends on the
' font. A Nerd Font devicon is one rune of one column with no ambiguity, so
' nerd is the decorated tier and ASCII is the tier that is correct everywhere.
'
' The node and wall glyphs are the BANNER's vocabulary rather than ASCII
' punctuation: the mark is dots interrupting rules inside a bracketed frame,
' and the figure is that idea applied to a run. It also ends a collision — "()"
' is the form's radio glyph three rows above, where it means something else.
'
' ── WHY THE COLUMNS TRAVEL AS ONE STRUCT ───────────────────────────────────
' topoCols is a struct rather than a handful of parameters because the columns
' must move TOGETHER: a hop column moved without the lanes column behind it
' draws the hop under the lanes, which is the one failure the fit budget exists
' to prevent. paneCols is the same figure in the margin, dropping the two
' things that cost columns and carry least — the connector runs shrink to a
' single rule (the arrowhead carries the direction, the length carries nothing)
' and the caption keeps only its leading clause.
'
' A label outside the frame is centred on its column and CLAMPED to the
' figure's origin: one wider than twice its column would otherwise start left
' of the figure and paint into its neighbour.
'
' The rule runs on to the fan so the lanes are visibly the hop's rather than
' three runs floating off to its right — and only when there ARE lanes, because
' a rule running to an empty margin would draw the route the tier is
' withholding. The animation mote REPLACES a rule rather than joining the run,
' so a lane is the same width moving as at rest; on the pane, where a run is
' one rule wide, the mote IS the lane for that frame.
'
' ── THE BUDGET'S EXACT ARITHMETIC ──────────────────────────────────────────
' Five rows and 72 columns are the strip's fixed geometry. Five because a
' figure clipped mid-body is worse than one that is absent; 72 because that is
' already the width the add-on divider centres on, so carrying the strip gives
' the form no new minimum width.
'
' THE PROMISE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. draw() has always painted straight down
' and tcell has always discarded the overflow, so a terminal shorter than the
' form still loses its last rows. What the budget guarantees is only that THE
' STRIP is never what pushed them off.
'
' The mandatory set is the title pair, the WHOLE body, the blank-and-hint pair,
' and the help slot with its opening blank. The body is charged at its FULL
' height, not its floor, so a decoration can only be bought with rows that are
' genuinely spare. Charging the floor was a real bug: the ladder then spent
' rows the body was already using, and GROWING the terminal by one row could
' buy the header and start the rows scrolling. "What a short terminal loses is
' decoration" has to hold in that direction too.
'
' Only when even the mandatory regions do not fit does the body give ground —
' which is what scrolling is for. That ordering is also what makes the foot
' FOLLOW the body rather than sit at the screen's bottom edge, so a form that
' fits renders exactly where it always did.
'
' ── THE HELP RESERVATION SPANS THE TICK, NOT JUST THE OPTION ───────────────
' maxHelpLines is taken over every row AND every option, and over the tick
' state too: helpLines writes "off: " for a greyed box and "always on: " for a
' greyed-and-ticked one, six columns apart, so a space press could still wrap
' one line further and move the figure — the exact jump the reservation exists
' to stop.
'
' ── WIDTH IS MEASURED THE PAINTER'S WAY, NOT THE RIGHT WAY ─────────────────
' rightEdge advances by the same len() drawBodyLine uses — BYTES, not display
' columns. Agreeing with the painter matters more than being right about wide
' runes: a non-ASCII option label already mis-advances the paint, and a budget
' that quietly "fixed" it would disagree with where the options actually land
' and let the pane overwrite one.
'
' A heading's real right edge is not the 72 it is centred ON: the painter
' writes 6 rules, the padded label and 6 more from a pad of (72-L)/2, so a
' label past ~48 reaches beyond 72 — and the heading branch ignores the limit.
'
' THE INLINE REASON IS MEASURED IN FULL, never clipped to a floor. A floor was
' worse than no budget: the locked sbx egress row carries changeBaselineHint —
' the one runnable command for changing the host baseline — with no Help and no
' OffWhy, and a locked row can never hold the cursor, so the help block never
' shows it either. Clipping destroyed the only copy of that text to make room
' for a picture, which is precisely the trade the pane may not make.
'
' ── THE PANE IS PLACED, NEVER CLIPPED INTO PLACE ───────────────────────────
' paneOrigin is chosen AFTER the widest row this form has, so no option is ever
' cut to make room for a picture: the shipped invariant holds on the width axis
' too. It is anchored RIGHT rather than flush against the rows — both satisfy
' the budget, but hugging the rows leaves the margin empty on a wide terminal
' while still pressing on the row limit, whereas against the right edge the
' rows keep every column the figure does not need.
title Choice-prompt topology strip — one projection, one budget, one clock

node "internal/choiceui" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "layout(w, h)\nfit budget · drop ladder · body height" as LAYOUT <<app>>
  component "Form.Topology(f, cursor)\nrun/topology.go builds one Frame" as MODEL <<app>>
  component "drawFigure(s, x0, y0, cols, ...)\none painter, block or pane" as PAINT <<app>>
  component "glyphsFor(tier)\nnerd devicons · ascii" as GLYPH <<app>>
  component "topoCols\nblockCols · paneCols" as COLS <<app>>
}

component "draw(screen, cursor)\nexisting paint, now budget-aware" as DRAW <<app>>
component "Run()\nexisting event loop" as RUN <<app>>
component "Rows\negress · credentials · auth\nexecution · interface · agent evidence" as ROWS <<app>>
queue "animTicker\nPostEvent every ~120ms, while unsettled" as TICK <<async>>
component "Operator terminal\nw x h, resized at will" as TTY <<host>>

component "A figure that only decorates" as DECOR <<error>>
component "A figure that disagrees with the boxes" as LIE <<error>>

ROWS ARROW_MAIN MODEL : the same values ""Selection"" returns
MODEL ARROW_MAIN PAINT : Frame, rebuilt per paint
GLYPH ARROW_MAIN PAINT
COLS ARROW_MAIN PAINT : one painter, two column sets
TTY ARROW_MAIN LAYOUT : width x height, on every resize
LAYOUT ARROW_MAIN DRAW : which regions are affordable
DRAW ARROW_OPTIONAL PAINT : only when the strip fits
PAINT ARROW_MAIN TTY
TICK ARROW_QUEUE RUN : EventInterrupt — the clock is an event
RUN ARROW_MAIN DRAW
RUN ARROW_MAIN TICK : started on the first change, stopped before ""Fini""
PAINT ARROW_ERROR DECOR
MODEL ARROW_ERROR LIE

note top of MODEL
  **The strip is a projection, never a second
  state.** It reads the rows ""Selection"" reads,
  so a frame cannot disagree with the checkboxes
  above it — which is the failure every ASCII
  diagram bolted onto a form eventually reaches.
end note

note bottom of MODEL
  **The cursor picks the emphasis, not the
  content.** Every element is drawn on every
  frame; the focused row raises one to brand and
  dims the rest, so nothing appears or vanishes
  as the operator walks down the form.
end note

note right of ROWS
  **Six rows, four targets.** ""egress"" owns the
  lanes and clouds — and is LOCKED on sbx, where
  it reports a host fact — ""credentials"" and
  ""auth"" own the key, ""execution"" the square,
  ""interface"" the return, evidence the glyph.
end note

note top of LAYOUT
  **The figure outranks the banner.** This prompt
  is about posture and the figure is the only
  thing on it that draws posture, so eight rows
  of wordmark go before five rows of it. The
  rows, hint and help slot are never dropped.
end note

note bottom of LAYOUT
  **The help slot is reserved at its maximum.**
  Help is 0-4 lines and changes with the cursor,
  so a strip drawn straight after it would jump
  a row per keystroke. The reserved slot is the
  only thing that holds the figure still.
end note

note bottom of LAYOUT
  **The ladder is strict, not best-fit.** A
  cheaper region may not sneak past a dearer one
  that was refused — which is what stopped the
  figure vanishing as the terminal GREW, back
  when the budget was a best fit.
end note

note bottom of LAYOUT
  **The pane is not a rung.** A region that spends
  no height cannot be refused by a budget it never
  draws on, so it is decided BEFORE the ladder and
  the ladder it falls through to is the shipped
  one, run without the strip rung.
end note

note bottom of COLS
  **A fidelity, not a fourth drawing.** It drops
  the connector runs, review's prose and the
  caption's gloss — the three things that cost
  columns and carry least. Every fact survives:
  the key's home, the labels, the lane counts.
end note

note bottom of COLS
  **The figure is the mark's own shape.** Dots
  interrupting rules inside a bracketed frame is
  what the banner is; the run is drawn the same
  way, so the two read as one drawing and "()"
  keeps meaning "a radio" everywhere else.
end note

note top of PAINT
  **What runs INSIDE the agent is drawn inside
  the frame.** The harness and the interface it
  drives are not peers of the host — they are
  what the container holds — and drawing them
  there costs a row less than labelling them out.
end note

note bottom of PAINT
  **A node outside the frame names itself on the
  row beneath its dot.** That is what makes the
  figure seven rows rather than five, and it is
  why the return to the host is an elbow: the
  label sits between the dot and the line.
end note

note top of COLS
  **The breakpoint is derived, never a constant.**
  It sits past THIS form's widest row — reasons
  and labels included — so nothing is cut for a
  picture. A plain form reaches it near 150; one
  with a long inline reason, past 200.
end note

note top of COLS
  **A short form gets no pane at all.** It is
  refused unless the body can hold the figure's
  four rows, so the fidelity that costs no height
  is still not free of the form: there has to be
  something for it to sit beside.
end note

note top of TICK
  **""PollEvent"" blocks, so the clock has to be
  an event.** A goroutine posting an interrupt
  at ~8 fps is the only tick that does not
  starve the keystrokes the form waits on.
end note

note bottom of TICK
  **Motion is bound to change, not to time.**
  A keystroke animates for ~1.2s and the strip
  then rests on a still frame, so an idle prompt
  costs no CPU and any movement means something
  in the form just moved.
end note

note bottom of TICK
  **Stopped before ""Fini"", on every path.** A
  ticker outliving the screen posts into a dead
  ""tcell.Screen""; accept and cancel both return
  through ""Run"", so the stop belongs on the
  defer already standing beside it.
end note

note top of GLYPH
  **No emoji reach the terminal.** The speaking
  head is two runes that ""go-runewidth"" measures
  as ONE column and terminals draw as two, and
  the cloud is ambiguous-width. A devicon is one
  rune of one column, so nerd is the top tier.
end note

note bottom of GLYPH
  **""put"" advances one column per rune**, so
  the strip has its own ""pen"". ""off"" draws the
  ASCII figure rather than none: it says the
  terminal cannot decorate, not that the reader
  wants less than the boxes can tell them.
end note

note bottom of DECOR
  **A figure that only decorates earns no rows.**
  It must answer what the checkboxes cannot —
  where the key sleeps, what the tier stops —
  or the five rows it costs belong to the form
  that is actually being filled in.
end note

note bottom of LIE
  **There is no single egress axis.** A sbx
  harness shows the host baseline — allow-all,
  balanced, deny-all — and a docker one shows
  proveo's tiers. Different question, different
  hop; a frame must name which it drew.
end note

' --- IGNORE: layout hints (do not edit) ---
DECOR -down[hidden]- LIE
' --- END IGNORE ---
@enduml
